<svelte:head><title>Privacy and participation — Voynich@home</title><meta name="description" content="Learn what Voynich@home stores for guest and account participation, public profiles, deletion, backups, and software checks."/></svelte:head>
<div class="page narrow prose"><p class="eyebrow">Last updated · 5 September 2026</p><h1>Privacy and participation</h1><p class="lede">Computation starts only after your click. Accounts and public recognition are optional.</p><h2>Guest participation</h2><p>An HTTP-only, same-site cookie identifies your guest session for up to 90 days; it is marked secure on HTTPS. The server stores a hash of its proof rather than the cookie value. Checkpoints and unsent results use IndexedDB storage in your browser. Clearing browser data removes your ability to resume that work.</p><h2>Accounts and public profiles</h2><p>If you sign in, the authentication service stores your provider account, name, email, sessions, and encrypted provider tokens when returned. Session records can include your IP address and browser information. Contribution rankings do not publish these details.</p><p>Your display name, checked credit, and team membership become public only after you choose public visibility. Team names are public. Guest contributions attach to an account only when the current browser proves control of that guest session.</p><h2>Deletion and research records</h2><p>Account deletion removes the account, sign-in sessions, and public profile. It detaches contribution records and revokes attached guest-session proofs. Research records remain without your account identity so published experiments can still be reproduced.</p><h2>Operating data</h2><p>The hosting provider processes network requests and operational logs. For short-lived abuse limits, the application converts an IP address into a different daily digest and also counts requests by guest session. The operator can inspect work failures and moderation records. The project uses no advertising trackers and does not sell contribution profiles.</p><p>Daily backups are retained for 30 days and can contain earlier account records during that period. A restored backup must reapply deletion records before public access resumes.</p><h2>Software trust</h2><p>Approved worker modules have published digests and restricted imports. These checks detect mismatched code, but a compromised website could replace the checking code itself. A verified native release provides a separate reproduction path.</p><p><a href="/account">Manage sessions, visibility, or account deletion →</a></p><p class="small muted">Questions or privacy requests can be filed through the project’s <a href="https://github.com/javidmardanov/VoynichAtHome/issues">GitHub issue tracker ↗</a>.</p></div>
